  "hinge": "§7:84 is more administratively precise than it is usually given credit for. It sets a **standard of proof** — *\"if they can show (prove) that they are persecuted and their body and life have been threatened\"* — and then names the obligations that follow: take in, feed and clothe, offer safety **and activity (work)**. That is, in modern vocabulary, a status determination plus reception plus labour-market access. The verse does not prohibit a state from removing people who fail that test; it prohibits leaving them helpless, and it locates the decision in an assessment of the person rather than in the convenience of the state doing the removing. The hinge is therefore to **make the assessment portable and the obligations enforceable**. **First,** publish the **determination standard** used to select transferees — who is screened, by whom, against what evidence, with what appeal — because a scheme whose eligibility criteria are unpublished cannot be distinguished from a scheme for moving inconvenient people. **Second,** put the Rwandan integration commitments — residence, education, healthcare, labour market, courts — into a **treaty with third-party monitoring and a named reviewer**, not a joint statement, because a right that exists only in a press release is not a right, and the five governments know it. **Third,** publish the **first-year cohort's outcomes** — how many remained, how many were returned home, how many left the country unrecorded — because those three numbers, not the signing ceremony, are what the pilot is testing, and by the time they exist the political argument will have moved on. §11:62 of the same book puts the principle beyond argument: neither *standing (rank) nor name nor goods and riches*, nor *the colour of skin, a belief… descent from people (race)* are of any significance as worth. A hub that meets that standard would be defensible on its merits. The one announced this week has not yet shown that it does, and the burden of showing it belongs to the five states, not to the people being moved."
